@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title `proveo clean` — reclaim leaked run state without killing live runs

actor "Maintainer" as MT

node "Host" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "cmd/proveo clean\ngathers inventory · executes plan" as CMD <<app>>
  component "internal/clean\nBuildPlan(inv, opts) — pure" as CORE <<app>>

  frame "Inventory" as INV <<host>> {
    component "egress session containers\n(mitm · squid · ollama)" as C <<app>>
    component "legacy DinD sidecars\n(retired; never session-labeled)" as D <<app>>
    component "egress networks\n+ HasEndpoints" as N <<app>>
    database "<stateDir>/egress/<sid>\nholds injected broker.env" as ST <<error>>
    database "proveo/* images" as IMG <<db>>
    database "PROVEO_HOME (~/.proveo)\nsessions + seeded config" as PH <<db>>
    database "provisioned toolchains\n""toolchains/<os>-<arch>/"" per platform\n""\.local/share/mise"" · ""\.go"" · ""go"" · shims" as TOOLS <<db>>
  }
}

MT ARROW_MAIN CMD
CMD ARROW_MAIN CORE : Inventory + Options
CORE ARROW_MAIN CMD : Plan{remove…, SkippedLive}

CORE ARROW_MAIN C : routine — dead sessions only
CORE ARROW_MAIN D : routine
CORE ARROW_MAIN N : routine — only when no endpoints
CORE ARROW_ERROR ST : routine — successLabel(wipes broker.env)
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL IMG : ""--deep"" only
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL PH : ""--homes"" only
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL TOOLS : ""--tools"" only

note bottom of TOOLS
  **A cache, held to a stricter liveness rule than everything else.** The
  toolchain tree is what makes install-once/run-many work: language servers, the
  Go toolchain and the jdtls JDK land under PROVEO_HOME, so a warm run installs
  nothing. Nothing else prunes it, and it only grows.

  **One tree per platform, plus the legacy one.** ""_proveo_tool_home"" writes to
  ""PROVEO_HOME/toolchains/<os>-<arch>"" so the docker backend and the sbx VM can
  SHARE a tree without an amd64 install answering ""command -v"" for an arm64
  sandbox. ""toolRoots"" globs those and keeps PROVEO_HOME itself in the list:
  trees installed before the relocation are still on operators' disks, and a
  flag that silently stopped reclaiming them would be worse than the growth.

  Per-session ephemera belong to exactly one run, so liveness is decided per
  session. This tree is shared by every run on the machine, so ANY running
  proveo container holds the whole prune back — ""--force"" is the documented
  escape hatch. Removing it is never destructive: the cost of being wrong is
  time on the next boot, not lost work, which is why it reports what it
  reclaims and why it is opt-in rather than part of ""--deep"".
end note

note right of CORE
  **Pure decision core.** ""BuildPlan"" performs no I/O:
  ""cmd/proveo"" gathers the inventory and executes. That
  is what makes the liveness rules unit-testable without
  Docker.

  **Liveness is the invariant.** A running container, or
  a network that still has endpoints, is left alone and
  reported in ""SkippedLive"" — a concurrent ""proveo run""
  must survive a clean. ""--force"" is the only override.
end note

note bottom of ST
  Wiping egress state is a **security** action, not just
  disk hygiene: a crashed run can leave the injected
  ""broker.env"" (the real provider key) on disk. Routine
  clean removes it.
end note

note bottom of PH
  Deliberately excluded from both routine and ""--deep"":
  it is the user's durable session history, not run
  ephemera. Upstream sidecar bases (squid, ollama) are
  likewise left — shared and cheap to re-pull. See _spec/internal/proveohome/proveo-home-components.puml
end note
note bottom of CORE
  **The decision core is pure: an inventory in, a removal set out.** That is
  what lets the two tiers be asserted without Docker present.

  **"Live" is defined by a running container, and it protects the whole
  session.** When any of a session's egress containers is still running,
  its networks //and// its state directory are preserved — the state dir
  holds the injected secret and the flow record a live run is still
  writing. Anything skipped for that reason is reported rather than
  silently kept, so an operator who expected a reclaim learns why it did
  not happen.

  Routine covers per-run ephemera left by a crashed or killed run — egress
  session containers and networks, session state dirs, and
  ""proveo-dind-*"" sidecars from before that sidecar was retired
  (_spec/_plans/retire-dind.puml). Nothing starts one now, and the sweep
  is kept precisely for that reason: a ""--privileged"" container an older
  proveo left running is the last thing to strand on a host with nothing
  left that knows its name. The deeper tier is opt-in because it reaches
  images the next run would otherwise reuse.
end note


@enduml
